## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (Python with pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib; nothing is
installed). Both inputs are resolved by `resolve_artifact.py` at execution time and read-only.

1. Read the `counts_gene` matrix (columns `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per sample)
   and the `design` table (`sample_id,condition,group,replicate`). Require that the sample
   columns and the design's `sample_id` values are the same set (6 samples: A1-A3, B1-B3);
   stop with an error otherwise. Samples are matched by name, never by position.
2. Filter genes: keep genes with at least 10 counts in at least 3 samples (the size of the
   smallest condition group).
3. Normalise: scale each sample to counts-per-million by its library size (column sum of the
   filtered matrix), then transform to log2(CPM + 1). The input is the length-scaled count
   matrix, which is non-integer, so a variance-stabilising transform that needs integer counts
   is not used.
4. Select the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples on the log2(CPM + 1) scale
   (all remaining genes if fewer than 500).
5. Centre each gene to mean zero across samples; do not scale to unit variance.
6. Fit `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` on the samples x genes matrix (`svd_solver="full"`), keeping
   all components (at most 5 with 6 samples).
7. Write the sample scores, the variance explained per component, and a figure with two panels,
   PC1 vs PC2 and PC2 vs PC3, one colour per `condition` level, points labelled with sample ID,
   axis labels giving percent variance explained.
8. Print the number of genes before and after filtering, so the filter's effect is in the job log.

The parameters (count and sample thresholds, log2 CPM, top 500 genes, no scaling) are choices
made for this plan; change them here before approval if you want others.

